# the-ground — plan 25 M3's headline fixture: a world that FILLS the frame.
#
# ⚠⚠ The filling is the whole design of this file.  M3's gate says
# `other == 0` — no pixel that is not a palette colour — and a 3-D frame
# of a finite world is mostly BACKGROUND, which is not a palette colour
# by deliberate choice (`plans/25` § Open 5: make the clear colour a
# palette colour and an erased-gap hole becomes invisible to the gate).
# So the camera has to look at nothing but ground, and that means the
# paint has to reach past the frustum.
#
# At the overview's near boom (20 m, 89°, 60° fov, 4:3) the ground plane
# spans about ±11.5 m by ±15.4 m, which is r ±11 and q ±12.  Painted to
# q ±22 and r ±14, so a camera change has to be large before the gate
# starts measuring the coastline instead of the ground.
#
# ⚠ The two LANDMARKS are the other half.  A per-kind pixel count is
# permutation-invariant — a MIRRORED world has the identical counts — so
# the gate also asks where two uniquely-coloured hexes landed and
# compares that to `camera_screen`'s prediction.  They are:
#
#   sand at (-7,  7)   the only kind-4 hex in the world
#   rock at ( 7, -5)   the only kind-7 hex in the world
#
# Both are off-axis and on opposite sides of the origin, so no
# reflection and no quarter turn leaves both in place.  ⚠ Move either
# and `src/gl_gate.loft` must move with it — it names them.
#
# ⚠⚠ **And both are FLAT, which is not a detail.**  The gate compares
# the CENTROID of a colour with the projected centre of that hex's top
# face, and a column that STANDS also draws its sides in the same
# colour — sides that sit between the top face and the screen centre,
# because a camera above the middle of the frame sees the inward face of
# an off-centre column.  Measured: `wall_high` at (-5, 3) came out
# **29 px** from its top face's centre for exactly that reason, where a
# 1 m rubble pile came out 2.3 px.  A landmark at ground level in
# ground-level surroundings emits no side quad at all (`@X046`), so its
# centroid IS its centre and the tolerance can stay tight enough to mean
# something.  ⚠ Loosening the tolerance instead would have hidden the
# geometry rather than measured it.

echo the-ground: a frame-filling world with two landmarks

at 0 0 1

# ── The ground.  One drag per row; 29 rows of 45. ──
palette 5
drag -22 -14 22 -14
drag -22 -13 22 -13
drag -22 -12 22 -12
drag -22 -11 22 -11
drag -22 -10 22 -10
drag -22  -9 22  -9
drag -22  -8 22  -8
drag -22  -7 22  -7
drag -22  -6 22  -6
drag -22  -5 22  -5
drag -22  -4 22  -4
drag -22  -3 22  -3
drag -22  -2 22  -2
drag -22  -1 22  -1
drag -22   0 22   0
drag -22   1 22   1
drag -22   2 22   2
drag -22   3 22   3
drag -22   4 22   4
drag -22   5 22   5
drag -22   6 22   6
drag -22   7 22   7
drag -22   8 22   8
drag -22   9 22   9
drag -22  10 22  10
drag -22  11 22  11
drag -22  12 22  12
drag -22  13 22  13
drag -22  14 22  14

# ── A wall, deliberately NOT symmetric. ──
#
# A ring would be its own mirror image, and the landmarks would then be
# the only thing standing between the gate and a reflected world.  This
# one has a long SOUTH face (r = -7 is the smallest row, and r grows
# NORTH — `camera.loft` § pan north is `r += 1`), two returns of unequal
# length, and an open north-east corner.
palette 9
drag -9 -7  9 -7
drag  9 -7  9  0
drag -9 -7 -9  4
drag -9  4 -2  4

# ── Two more structure heights, for the per-kind counts to see. ──
#
# ⚠ Content, not landmarks: a 5 m column and a 1 m pile are exactly the
# things whose drawn area is NOT centred on their top face, so they are
# gated by pixel COUNT and never by position.
palette 10
click -5 3

raise 7 -6 1.0 wreckage

# ── The landmarks — flat, unique, and on opposite sides. ──
palette 4
click -7 7

palette 7
click 7 -5

# ── What the SOFTWARE gate can already see about this world. ──
#
# ⚠ These are not the GL measurements — `.keys` has no GL verb and
# deliberately does not (a frame command that needed a context would put
# every one of the 33 headless scripts behind an X server).  They are
# here so this fixture is a real scenario in its own right: play it with
# `validate.sh` and the world is checked before anything is drawn.
kind   0   0 grass
kind  -5   3 wall_high
kind   9  -3 wall
kind  -9  -7 wall
kind  23   0 sea
kind   0  15 sea

# The landmarks.  ⚠ That each is the ONLY hex of its kind is what makes
# a centroid mean "where that hex is" — and `.keys` has no per-kind
# census verb, so `src/gl_gate.loft` asserts it where it can actually be
# seen: a second sand hex anywhere in the frame would roughly double the
# colour's PIXEL COUNT, which the landmark check bands.
kind  -7   7 sand
kind   7  -5 rock

# ⚠ The rubble landmark asserts BOTH halves, and the pair is the point.
# `kind` reads the AUTHORED ground and a pile is a LAYER over it
# (`CLAUDE.md` § Rubble is a LAYER, never a repaint), so the hex under
# the heap is still grass — while the mesher colours it by the SURFACE
# and draws it violet.  Assert only the pile and a mesher that lost the
# surface lookup still passes here; assert only the ground and the
# landmark below has nothing standing behind it.
kind   7  -6 grass
pile   7  -6 1.0 1.0
